---
// Lesson 3.4 — Projectile Damage, Tuning & Playtest
// Closes the turret loop. The student discovers Turret_Projectile.prefab has
// NO Collider2D and NO Rigidbody2D (the same discovery as 2.1), adds what their
// chosen setup requires, detects the hit, applies damage through the health
// method THEY built on PlayerController in 2.2, and destroys the bullet. Then
// they define their own difficulty knobs and playtest.
//
// Scaffolding: OUTLINE ONLY (level 3). The spec and the API are given and no
// code is. The student built the collider-and-tag pickup in 2.1, the signed
// damage method in 2.2, the spawn-then-destroy lifecycle in 2.4 and 3.2, and
// the cooldown in 3.3 — so this lesson names the file, the callback, the
// decision and the cost, and stops there. The ask banner is the level marker:
// a level-3 lesson must not render a walkthrough, and verify-site refuses one
// that does.
//
// GROUND TRUTH: StatManager.cs is still an EMPTY STUB - it is not used here.
// Health lives as a field on PlayerController, where the student put it in 2.2,
// and the health method there is private; making it callable from another
// script is part of today's work. Two Turret instances already exist in the
// Overworld, so three personalities costs one duplicate. The difficulty values
// are the student's own fields, not values that ship with the project.
//
// STRUCTURE (this pass, "teach it once"): ONE editor block and ONE code block.
// The trigger-versus-solid decision is a checkbox, so it lives in the editor
// steps and the callbacks it chooses between are a single API row. The clicks
// that can only happen after the fields exist are the last steps of the same
// list, marked "Once the code half is done" (the 3.1 pattern), rather than a
// second labelled section. Culled as repetition: the whole second socratic
// block and its "Read Function-Level Examples" activity, the separate
// difficulty-tuning editor block (its four knobs are named once, in the step
// that sets them), the standalone StatManager callout (folded into a spec
// bullet, where the same claim was already being made), the four-row
// difficulty table, and the paragraph introducing each section.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted, so the surviving socratic block
// keeps its three questions in order (reworded, never moved) and the decompose
// block keeps both of its turns (dc1:q2, dc1:q3) where they were.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// Level 3, so the renderer withholds what each part DOES and asks the student to
// say it — the parts and their doc pointers are the level-3 bargain. Every doc
// pointer is a lesson they have finished or a row of this lesson's own API
// table.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big: 'My bullets should actually hurt the player, and the fight should be hard without being unfair.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Give the bullet a body',
      does: 'The prefab ships as a Transform and a Sprite Renderer and nothing else. Add the collider your chosen setup needs, size it against the scaled sprite, and add the Rigidbody2D that setup requires: a message needs a collider on both objects and a body on at least one of them.',
      doc: '2.1 — Coins: Triggers, Tags & Your First Stat (the collider-and-trigger recipe), and the Rigidbody2D row of this lesson\'s API table',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Detect the hit, and confirm what was hit',
      does: 'A callback Unity calls on the frame the two shapes meet, plus the tag check that separates the player from a wall, the turret that fired you, or another bullet.',
      doc: 'the OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other), OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision) and other.CompareTag("Player") rows of this lesson\'s API table',
    },
    {
      name: 'Apply the damage through the health method you already wrote',
      does: 'Reach the PlayerController on the object you hit with a component lookup, then call the signed-amount method from 2.2 with the sign that hurts, so the one method still serves both damage and healing.',
      doc: '2.2 — Spikes: Physics, Damage & Death (the method you wrote there), and the GetComponent<T>() row of this lesson\'s API table',
      href: '2.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Remove the bullet',
      does: 'Remove the bullet so one shot costs one hit — the same cleanup call 3.2 made on a timer, now triggered by a hit instead of a clock.',
      doc: '3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate(), Fire, Destroy(), and the Destroy(Object obj) row of this lesson\'s API table',
      href: '3.2.html',
    },
  ],
  why: 'Three of these four parts are lessons you have already finished, and the fourth is the one genuinely new idea: until now, every script you wrote changed its own values. A bullet has to reach into a different object and ask that object to change its own number, which is a question about access, not about arithmetic.',
  sizeTest: 'The split is finished when every part names something you could point at in the editor: a component on a prefab, a callback, a method on a script, a call. If a part needs the word "and" twice to describe it, it is two parts.',
  turns: [
    'Trigger or solid — which did you choose for the bullet, and what does that choice cost you? Name the checkbox and the callback name that have to agree, and what the Console does when they do not.',
    'The projectile prefab is tagged `Enemy`, the same tag the turret that fired it carries. What will your callback actually test for, and on which object does it ask?',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>3.4 — Projectile Damage, Tuning &amp; Playtest</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your turret shoots, and every bullet passes clean through you. Close that loop, then set the
    four numbers that decide whether the fight is tense or unfair.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make my bullet hurt the player?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('3.4', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code>, then
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> a Transform and a Sprite Renderer and nothing else. Note the
            sprite's <strong>Scale</strong>: a collider sized from the import bounds will not match
            it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Add Component → Circle Collider 2D</strong> (or Box Collider 2D),
            then <strong>Inspector → Circle Collider 2D → Radius</strong>: drag until the green
            outline covers the art in the <strong>Scene view</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Is Trigger</strong> decides your callback: ticked, the bullet reports
            the overlap and passes into you; unticked, it is solid and shoves a Dynamic player. Then
            <strong>Inspector → Add Component → Rigidbody 2D</strong> if your setup needs one —
            <strong>Body Type = Dynamic</strong>, <strong>Gravity Scale = 0</strong>, because this
            world has no "down".
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/Player</code>, then
            <strong>Inspector → Tag</strong>: the exact spelling your callback will test, on the
            object carrying the collider, with the body from 2.2. The pair rule runs this project —
            a collider on both objects, a body on at least one.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the code half is done — Hierarchy →</strong> a <strong>Turret</strong>
            instance, duplicate it (<strong>Ctrl+D</strong>) so the Overworld carries three, then
            <strong>Inspector → Turret Controller →</strong> each instance's own range and cooldown
            (both in <code>TurretController.cs</code>), the projectile's own speed
            (<code>Projectile.cs</code>) and damage (today's script). An instance's values override
            the prefab's, which is how three copies of one prefab become three enemies.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view → press Play</strong> and stand in front of a turret: health falls by
            exactly your damage knob, the bullet vanishes, and at zero your 2.2 death rule fires.
            Console silent? Check the collider before the code, and the tag spelling before the
            collider.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it is</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code> or
                <code>OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision)</code> — one, matching your
                checkbox</td>
            <td>A callback. Unity calls it; you never do.</td>
            <td>The hit report, on the frame the two shapes meet.</td>
            <td>Set <em>Is Trigger</em> against the name and it never runs: a misspelt callback is
                silence, not an error.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>other.CompareTag("Player")</code></td>
            <td>Identity check by tag.</td>
            <td>One check, and you know what you hit.</td>
            <td>Exact spelling, and the tag must exist. Ask it of the object carrying the collider,
                never of the script the callback lives on.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>T GetComponent&lt;T&gt;()</code></td>
            <td>A component lookup on the object you call it on.</td>
            <td>The <code>PlayerController</code> that owns the health.</td>
            <td>A search that returns <code>null</code> when the type is not there, and the plain
                version does not look at children.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Calling a public method on another component —
                <code>player.TakeDamage(amount);</code></td>
            <td>A call on a reference to another script, named whatever you named yours in 2.2.</td>
            <td>Hands the problem to the object that owns the data: the bullet only asks.</td>
            <td>2.2's method is <code>private</code>, so nothing outside <code>PlayerController</code>
                can see it yet, and everything public becomes a promise to every future caller.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Destroy(Object obj)</code></td>
            <td>Removes an object at the end of the frame.</td>
            <td>The cleanup 3.2 used on a timer, now on a hit.</td>
            <td>It is final and deferred: the object lives out the frame, so a hit can be counted
                twice unless the callback stops itself running again.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for the hit script.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>The hit:</strong> which callback receives the message, and which property carries the tag for that one? Why is the tag check the first filter? Which lookup reaches the <code>PlayerController</code> on the object you hit — plain, <code>InParent</code>, or <code>InChildren</code> — and what guard protects the call when it hands back <code>null</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>The damage:</strong> which method do you call on the player, and which sign is the number you pass? Your 2.2 method is <code>private</code>: what has to change before another script can call it, and what type must your damage knob be to match it? Health lives on <code>PlayerController</code> — the empty <code>StatManager.cs</code> in <code>Assets/Scripts</code> has no health and no method to change one.</li>
            <li><strong>The cleanup:</strong> which object do you destroy, and does that line come before the damage or after it? 3.2 ended with this same call on a timer — why does one hit still need both a lifetime and a kill on contact?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
<li>The bullet asks the player to change its own health number rather than changing it from outside. Which object owns that number, and what would the bullet have to know about the player that it does not need to know today?</li>
<li>Tick <em>Is Trigger</em> and the bullet reports; leave it off and it shoves. Which did you choose, and what does the player feel in each case? Name the checkbox and the callback that have to agree.</li>
<li>The projectile prefab is tagged <code>Enemy</code>, like the turret and the mobile enemy. Given that, how will a bullet ever know it hit the <em>player</em> — whose tag will you actually test, and on which object?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Playtest</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <ul>
        <li>
            Swap seats and run a silent playtest, <a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> rules: you watch, they
            play, nobody speaks. Did they notice the turret before it fired, or was the first hit a
            surprise? Did they try to dodge? Then name your fifth knob — a muzzle offset, a lifetime,
            a spread — declare it, and fix the top thing the playtest showed you.
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain what the projectile prefab was missing and why no message ever fired, and defend your choice of trigger versus solid by naming the callback it requires</li>
        <li>Explain how a bullet reaches the player's health method, why the lookup can hand back <code>null</code>, and what the guard does about it</li>
        <li>Name the four difficulty knobs, the file each one lives in, and your value for each</li>
        <li>Run a silent playtest and make one evidence-based change, then commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — the complete turret system</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the hit detection reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — triggers, tags, <code>GetComponent&lt;T&gt;()</code></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I&hellip;? index</a> — find this lesson by the question you were asking</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.1.html">4.1 — The Enemy: Patrol</a>, where Unit 4 begins and something starts hunting you.</p>
</div>`;
